    },
    {
      "id": "L47l0s4CixaM_Lc22fMfw",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2058.878892921785,
      "y": 1845.1254632437171,
      "width": 1366.3590087890625,
      "height": 2375,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4L",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1648795596,
      "version": 3931,
      "versionNonce": 1641543284,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1786771336452,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Prompting:\n    - a instruction and information provides to LLM for desired output\n    - an LLM can produce different answers depending on how you instruct it.\n    - Example: \n            You are a customer-support assistant.\n            \n            Answer the user's question using the provided context.\n            \n            Context:\n            {{context}}\n            \n            Question:\n            {{question}}\n    - System Prompt + Instructions + Constraints + Examples + User Query + Retrieved Context + Conversation Context ↓ LLM\n    - Common techniques:\n        Zero-shot = Instruction without examples\n        Few-shot = Instruction + examples\n        Role prompting = Define desired behavior/role\n        Prompting for structured output = Prompting for structured output\n        Prompt template = Reusable prompt with variables\n        Context injection = Add retrieved/user/application data\n        Output constraints = Specify format/length/style\n\n\n2. Context:\n    - information to LLM model \n    - User query + conversational history + Retrieved RAG docs + System/App instructions + Tools result + structured app data\n    - Context Window: amount of information of model can process for one request\n    - Context Engineering: designing what info model receives + when it receives + in what form\n        - It includes the : what info should keep, what conversational history should keep, How to order them , How to summarise \n    - Context compression: Reranking , deduplication, summarisation, extracting relevant sentences, contextual compression\n        - Reduce Token usage, low latency, improve answer quality\n    - Lost-in-the-middle: sometime important info was there in middle of large context and difficult\n        - use rerank the relevant chunks\n \n 3. Structured Output:\n    - LLM needs to return in predictable structure; Free-form generation → machine-readable output\n    - use schema-constrained structured output and examples\n    - schema: structure of output looks like\n        - CustomerAnalysis\n            ├── sentiment: string\n            ├── priority: integer\n            └── reason: string\n    - JSON mostly used, Classification, Extraction, Routing, Tool invocation -> directly to func/tool calling\n    - Tool calling is essentially structured output used to drive an action\n        {\n          \"name\": \"get_weather\",\n          \"arguments\": {\n            \"city\": \"Kochi\"\n          }\n        }\n    - Structed output for RAG = answer, score, citations\n                Use the context to answer the question.\n                Return:\n                - answer\n                - confidence\n                - supporting_sources\n\n    -          LLM OUTPUT\n                     │\n        ┌──────────┐\n        ↓            ↓             ↓\n     Format    Correctness  Safety\n        │            │            │\n   Structured  Evaluation   Guardrails\n    Output\n\n\n4. Hallucination:\n    - LLM provides info which is false, unsupported, fabricated often in way that sounds confident.\n    - Why do LLM's hallucination ? : LLM is trained to generate next text/token not guarantee truth.\n    - Common Causes:\n        1. Missing Information: if relevant context were not there , model still can answer generally.\n        2. Failure/Poor retrieval: bad retrieval -> wrong context -> wrong answer; retrieval quality directly effected generation quality.\n        3. Ambiguous question: if context is unclear, model may make assumptions.\n        4. Conflicting info: if model has conflicting evidence then model may choose incorrectly.\n            - example: Document A:\n                        Refunds available within 30 days.\n                        \n                        Document B:\n                        Refunds available within 60 days.\n\n        5. Model knowledge limitations: model might don't know of particular fact or outdated info.\n\n    - RAG can reduce the hallucinations but not eliminate it. But still it can failure - relevant chunks, ignore context by LLM, conflicting info\n    - RAG is grounding mechanism not gurantee of truth.\n    - Grounding: making the model answer depends on the reliable source of info(context). add an Instruction on prompt.\n    - Abstention: making model answer like \"i don't have info\", \"I don't have enough information to answer\"\n    - Techniques for reducing hallucinations:\n        1. Better retrieval -> lower hallucination risk\n        2. Strong grounding instructions\n        3. Citations: Ask system to identify source of each answer\n                - Citations make answers easier to verify.\n        4. Require evidence : Then you can evaluate whether the answer is actually supported\n        5. Output validaton",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Prompting:\n    - a instruction and information provides to LLM for desired output\n    - an LLM can produce different answers depending on how you instruct it.\n    - Example: \n            You are a customer-support assistant.\n            \n            Answer the user's question using the provided context.\n            \n            Context:\n            {{context}}\n            \n            Question:\n            {{question}}\n    - System Prompt + Instructions + Constraints + Examples + User Query + Retrieved Context + Conversation Context ↓ LLM\n    - Common techniques:\n        Zero-shot = Instruction without examples\n        Few-shot = Instruction + examples\n        Role prompting = Define desired behavior/role\n        Prompting for structured output = Prompting for structured output\n        Prompt template = Reusable prompt with variables\n        Context injection = Add retrieved/user/application data\n        Output constraints = Specify format/length/style\n\n\n2. Context:\n    - information to LLM model \n    - User query + conversational history + Retrieved RAG docs + System/App instructions + Tools result + structured app data\n    - Context Window: amount of information of model can process for one request\n    - Context Engineering: designing what info model receives + when it receives + in what form\n        - It includes the : what info should keep, what conversational history should keep, How to order them , How to summarise \n    - Context compression: Reranking , deduplication, summarisation, extracting relevant sentences, contextual compression\n        - Reduce Token usage, low latency, improve answer quality\n    - Lost-in-the-middle: sometime important info was there in middle of large context and difficult\n        - use rerank the relevant chunks\n \n 3. Structured Output:\n    - LLM needs to return in predictable structure; Free-form generation → machine-readable output\n    - use schema-constrained structured output and examples\n    - schema: structure of output looks like\n        - CustomerAnalysis\n            ├── sentiment: string\n            ├── priority: integer\n            └── reason: string\n    - JSON mostly used, Classification, Extraction, Routing, Tool invocation -> directly to func/tool calling\n    - Tool calling is essentially structured output used to drive an action\n        {\n          \"name\": \"get_weather\",\n          \"arguments\": {\n            \"city\": \"Kochi\"\n          }\n        }\n    - Structed output for RAG = answer, score, citations\n                Use the context to answer the question.\n                Return:\n                - answer\n                - confidence\n                - supporting_sources\n\n    -          LLM OUTPUT\n                     │\n        ┌──────────┐\n        ↓            ↓             ↓\n     Format    Correctness  Safety\n        │            │            │\n   Structured  Evaluation   Guardrails\n    Output\n\n\n4. Hallucination:\n    - LLM provides info which is false, unsupported, fabricated often in way that sounds confident.\n    - Why do LLM's hallucination ? : LLM is trained to generate next text/token not guarantee truth.\n    - Common Causes:\n        1. Missing Information: if relevant context were not there , model still can answer generally.\n        2. Failure/Poor retrieval: bad retrieval -> wrong context -> wrong answer; retrieval quality directly effected generation quality.\n        3. Ambiguous question: if context is unclear, model may make assumptions.\n        4. Conflicting info: if model has conflicting evidence then model may choose incorrectly.\n            - example: Document A:\n                        Refunds available within 30 days.\n                        \n                        Document B:\n                        Refunds available within 60 days.\n\n        5. Model knowledge limitations: model might don't know of particular fact or outdated info.\n\n    - RAG can reduce the hallucinations but not eliminate it. But still it can failure - relevant chunks, ignore context by LLM, conflicting info\n    - RAG is grounding mechanism not gurantee of truth.\n    - Grounding: making the model answer depends on the reliable source of info(context). add an Instruction on prompt.\n    - Abstention: making model answer like \"i don't have info\", \"I don't have enough information to answer\"\n    - Techniques for reducing hallucinations:\n        1. Better retrieval -> lower hallucination risk\n        2. Strong grounding instructions\n        3. Citations: Ask system to identify source of each answer\n                - Citations make answers easier to verify.\n        4. Require evidence : Then you can evaluate whether the answer is actually supported\n        5. Output validaton",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
